# PART 8 — CRITICAL APPOINTMENT CONFLICT PREVENTION

This requirement is mandatory.

The user is one medical representative and cannot attend two appointments simultaneously.

**No two planned HCP appointments may overlap.**

## 8.1 Global no-overlap rule

For appointments A and B:

`A.start < B.end AND B.start < A.end`

If this expression is true, the appointments overlap.

The optimizer must prevent this during scheduling.

The independent validator must reject any plan that contains an overlap.

Do not simply detect overlaps after presenting an invalid plan.

## 8.2 Same location, same time window

If multiple HCPs share the same verified physical location, schedule them consecutively.

Default transition buffer: zero minutes.

Example:

Clinic X:
Available 2:00 PM–6:00 PM.

HCP A: 2:00–2:15 PM.

HCP B: 2:15–2:30 PM.

HCP C: 2:30–2:45 PM.

Do not add unnecessary road travel between verified identical locations.

Recognize shared addresses and verified coordinates.

Do not assume that similar zone names prove two clinics are in the same building.

If locations are not verified, do not automatically group them as identical.

## 8.3 Different locations

When HCPs are at different clinics:

`Next visit start >= Previous visit end + road travel time + required transition buffer`

Use road-based travel estimates whenever available.

Waiting is permitted when useful, but must be included in duty-span calculations.

## 8.4 Same HCP, multiple clinics

Do not schedule the same HCP at two clinics simultaneously.

Do not count two clinics as two separate HCP visits by default.

## 8.5 Independent validator

After optimization, independently validate:

- Appointment overlap.
- Travel feasibility.
- Clinic availability.
- Visit duration.
- Same-location sequential scheduling.
- Locked appointments.
- Duty-span limit.
- Split-shift prohibition.
- Duplicate qualifying visits.
- Return-home time when enabled.

Reject invalid plans and re-optimize.

If a plan is infeasible, explain the actual conflict.

